% Capacitor field — a library example (run it with F5; "Make a copy" to change it)
%
% Two parallel plates, w = 4 mm wide and d = 1 mm apart, at +0.5 V and −0.5 V in air, seen end-on (2-D: the plates
% are long in z). poisson2d solves for the potential in a 16 × 16 mm box; the canvas shows the potential and the
% field lines bending round the plate edges (fringing). The capacitance per metre of plate length comes from the
% stored energy, C' = 2W/V², and is compared with the ideal ε0 w/d (no fringing) and Palmer's fringing formula.
% Plotted: the potential along the vertical centre line — flat-sloped between the plates, tailing off outside.
% Units: mm (like the part on the canvas), so ε0 is in F/mm and C' comes out per mm of plate length (× 1000 per m).
clf                                    % start with an empty canvas and dock
e0 = 8.8541878128e-15;                 % F/mm
w = 4; d = 1; V = 1; box = 16; h = d / 8;
n = round(box / h);
m = quadGrid(box, box, n, n, [-box/2 -box/2]);
X = meshNodes(m);
tol = h / 100;
top = find(abs(X(:, 2) - d/2) < tol & abs(X(:, 1)) <= w/2 + tol);
bot = find(abs(X(:, 2) + d/2) < tol & abs(X(:, 1)) <= w/2 + tol);
bc = struct();
bc.fixed = {struct('nodes', top, 'value', V/2), struct('nodes', bot, 'value', -V/2)};
r = poisson2d(m, e0, 0, bc);
show(r.fields{2}, 'name', 'Capacitor: electric field (V/mm)');
C = 2 * r.energy / V^2 * 1000;        % F/m
C0 = e0 * w / d * 1000;
Cp = C0 * (1 + d / (pi * w) * (1 + log(2 * pi * w / d)));
fprintf('C'' = %.4g F/m: %.3f × the ideal ε0 w/d = %.4g F/m (fringing); Palmer''s formula %.3f × (%+.1f %%)\n', C, C / C0, C0, Cp / C0, 100 * (C / Cp - 1));
fprintf('charge on the + plate %.4g C/m (= C''·V %.4g)\n', r.charge(1) * 1000, C * V);
mid = find(abs(X(:, 1)) < tol);
[ys, o] = sort(X(mid, 2));
plot(ys', r.phi(mid(o))', 'title', 'Capacitor: potential on the centre line', 'xlabel', 'y (mm)', 'ylabel', 'φ (V)');
